tweet: "Climate and Conflict Collide as Indigenous Leaders Press the UN on Land Rights"
---
Indigenous leaders gathered at the United Nations this week, hundreds of delegates attending the 19th annual session of the UN Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, to address a compounding threat to Indigenous land rights and lives. The threat is not solely environmental, but a convergence of climate breakdown, armed conflict, state pressure, and legal marginalization that bear hardest on communities with the least capacity to absorb them. [1] This convergence is not incidental; climate breakdown, armed conflict, state pressure, and legal marginalization reinforce one another, exacerbating the damage they inflict on Indigenous communities.

## A Declaration and the Distance It Has to Travel

The UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, adopted nearly two decades ago by over 100 countries, was designed to protect the rights and lives of Indigenous peoples worldwide. However, the gap between this adoption and actual protection is where the current crisis is concentrated. Despite the declaration's existence, record-breaking storms still batter Indigenous lands, drought still desiccates them, and seas continue to rise, causing territories to vanish. The ICT News reporting highlights this disconnect, emphasizing that the declaration's existence has not paused any of these environmental disasters.

## Climate Is Not the Only Front

The climate threats documented in the ICT News report do not operate in isolation. The death toll, as characterized by Indigenous peoples, runs into the hundreds, spread across wars and acts of direct territorial defense. Government persecution compounds this picture further, adding to the pressure on Indigenous communities. All three forces â€“ climate breakdown, armed conflict, and state pressure â€“ are running simultaneously against the same communities, not sequenced across separate timelines or distinct geographies. This overlap is not coincidental; each pressure intensifies the others, exacerbating the damage inflicted on Indigenous communities.

"Climate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization reinforce one another," said Binota Moy Dhamai, a former chair of EMRIP and a representative from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh. This framing names the structural dynamic the Geneva gathering exists to address: not separate crises running in parallel, but interlocking pressures that compound each other's damage.

## What the Forum Makes Legible

Bringing these crises to the United Nations rather than to individual national capitals signals the scale at which Indigenous leaders see this problem â€“ and where they locate the failure of authority that shapes it. The 19th session exists explicitly to discuss implementing solutions to these abuses, not only to document them. A UN forum also serves as a record: what was said, by whom, and when. Whether this record translates into anything material depends on whether the states that adopted the declaration treat it as a constraint on their own development priorities and security decisions.

The crises described this week did not begin at the UN, and a forum alone does not end them. What this confrontation makes legible is the distance between a framework built to protect Indigenous land rights and the physical reality of those lands today â€“ a distance that grows as climate disruption accelerates.

The struggles of Indigenous Newar communities in Nepal, as documented in [Nepal's New Government's Fast Track Project Priority Sparks Conflict Over Indigenous Newar Lands in Khokana](https://www.culturalsurvival.org/news/nepals-new-governments-fast-track-project-priority-sparks-conflict-over-indigenous-newar-lands), illustrate the distance between the declaration and its implementation. The Nepali government's prioritization of infrastructure has put it in direct conflict with Indigenous Newar communities over their lands in Khokana, highlighting the need for a more inclusive development path that respects the inalienable human rights and heritage of these communities.

The challenges facing Indigenous peoples vary across regions, but the Geneva gathering aims to address several common themes, including the ongoing fight for Indigenous involvement in government decision-making. Many countries do not follow international standards, and there is widespread agreement among Indigenous peoples that they should be consulted and included in discussions that could impact them and their land.

